window.GIQ={"meta":{"lede":"Every labeled structure on the 24 bone models from the thorax and GI lab, as a “what is this?” question. All labels are covered and the yellow ? marks the one asked; pick its name from five look-alike choices. Each region opens with a short summary of the structures it covers. Each part has short notes, then its core questions (one per structure), then a “More questions” dropdown with the same structures on other pictures. After you answer: where it lies, what it comes from or drains into, what it is deep and superficial to, and more pictures of it.","feat":1,"fold":1,"tier":1,"uw":"Region","type":"bone","npic":24,"pic":0},"lectures":[{"id":"BON-T1","t":"Thoracic wall & bony thorax"},{"id":"BON-A1","t":"Abdominal wall"}],"groups":[{"l":"BON-T1","u":0,"label":"Manubrium (of sternum)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_107_1334.jpg","cap":"Sternum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.6","tb":1,"ov":[[0.4922,0.2783,0.6679,0.329]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s518~manubrium","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Manubrium (of sternum)","B. Costal notches for ribs 2-7","C. First rib","D. Body of sternum","E. Xiphoid process"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Manubrium (of sternum)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Manubrium (of sternum).</b> The manubrium is the top part of the sternum, between the clavicles at T3 to T4, with the jugular notch above and the left brachiocephalic vein behind it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin, the sternal heads of sternocleidomastoid and pectoralis major</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sternohyoid, sternothyroid, thymus remnant, left brachiocephalic vein, then the aortic arch branches</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the medial ends of the clavicles; the broad bone between them is the manubrium, ending below at the transverse ridge of the sternal angle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s518.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 518"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.5048,0.9455,1,0.9934,"",1]],"ar":1.73623}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":0,"label":"Jugular (suprasternal) notch","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_107_1334.jpg","cap":"Sternum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.6","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3925,0.0136,0.5867,0.0632]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s522~jugular-notch","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Jugular (suprasternal) notch","B. Costal notch for first rib","C. Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)","D. Xiphoid process","E. Clavicular notch of manubrium"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Jugular (suprasternal) notch","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Jugular (suprasternal) notch.</b> The jugular notch is the midline dip on top of the manubrium, between the two clavicular notches, level with T2 and with the trachea right behind it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and fascia only, between the sternal heads of the two sternocleidomastoids</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Trachea at the root of the neck, felt in the midline deep to it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the medial ends of both clavicles; the dip in the midline between them on top of the manubrium is the jugular notch.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s522.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 522"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.5048,0.9455,0.8471,0.9934,"",1]],"ar":1.73623}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":0,"label":"Clavicular notch of manubrium","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_107_1334.jpg","cap":"Sternum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.6","tb":1,"ov":[[0.796,0.0136,0.942,0.0953]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s520~clavicular-notch-of-manubrium","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Xiphoid process","B. Clavicular notch of manubrium","C. Costal notch for first rib","D. Costal notches for ribs 2-7","E. Jugular (suprasternal) notch"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Clavicular notch of manubrium","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Clavicular notch of manubrium.</b> The clavicular notches are the shallow sockets on each upper corner of the manubrium, beside the jugular notch, where the medial ends of the clavicles form the sternoclavicular joints.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior sternoclavicular ligament and the sternal head of sternocleidomastoid</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sternohyoid origin, then the brachiocephalic vein forming behind the sternoclavicular joint</dd></dl>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s520.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 520"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.5048,0.9455,1,0.9934,"",1]],"ar":1.73623}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":0,"label":"Costal notch for first rib","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_107_1334.jpg","cap":"Sternum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.6","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7419,0.419,0.8616,0.5006]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s524~costal-notch-for-first-rib","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Costal notch for first rib","B. Xiphoid process","C. Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)","D. Costal notches for ribs 2-7","E. Jugular (suprasternal) notch"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Costal notch for first rib","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Costal notch for first rib.</b> The costal notch for the first rib is the depression on the upper side edge of the manubrium, just below the clavicular notch, where the 1st costal cartilage attaches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s524.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 524"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.5048,0.9455,1,0.9934,"",1]],"ar":1.73623}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":0,"label":"Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_159_1439.jpg","cap":"Pericardial sac in relation to sternum and phrenic nerves","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.44","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7573,0.2512,0.955,0.295]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s526.jpg","cap":"Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 526","ov":[[0.5028,0.9435,0.8491,0.9954]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s061~sternal-angle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Costal notches for ribs 2-7","B. Xiphoid process","C. Jugular (suprasternal) notch","D. Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)","E. Costal notch for first rib"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint).</b> The sternal angle is the ridge at the joint between manubrium and sternal body where the second costal cartilages attach, level with the T4–T5 disc.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and pectoralis major origins; palpable ridge where the 2nd costal cartilages attach</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Plane of the T4-T5 disc: start and end of the aortic arch, tracheal bifurcation</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the jugular notch and slide down the manubrium to the first transverse ridge; the rib cartilage attached at that level is the second.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s061.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 61"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1009,0.9498,0.2771,0.9947,"",1]],"ar":0.94465}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":0,"label":"Xiphoid process","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_150_102.jpg","cap":"","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7954,0.7245,0.9545,0.7728]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s530.jpg","cap":"Xiphoid process on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 530","ov":[[0.5028,0.9435,0.8791,0.9954]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q27~xiphoid-process","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)","B. Jugular (suprasternal) notch","C. Costal notch for first rib","D. Xiphoid process","E. Clavicular notch of manubrium"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Xiphoid process","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Xiphoid process.</b> The xiphoid process is the small lowest part of the sternum, below the sternal body at the xiphisternal joint (level with T9), where the two costal margins meet.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Linea alba and rectus abdominis attach to its front; skin of the epigastric fossa</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sternal slips of the diaphragm and transversus thoracis, which attach to its back</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the costal margins converging in the midline; the small projection at their apex, below the body of the sternum, is the xiphoid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q27.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 27"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2653,0.9592,0.454,0.9966,"",1]],"ar":1.16214}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":0,"label":"Costal notches for ribs 2-7","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_107_1334.jpg","cap":"Sternum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.6","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7419,0.419,0.8616,0.5006]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s528~costal-notches-for-ribs-2-7","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)","B. Xiphoid process","C. Clavicular notch of manubrium","D. Costal notches for ribs 2-7","E. Costal notch for first rib"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Costal notches for ribs 2-7","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Costal notches for ribs 2-7.</b> The costal notches for ribs 2–7 line the side edges of the sternum: the 2nd at the sternal angle, the 3rd–6th on the body, the 7th at the xiphisternal junction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s528.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 528"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.5048,0.9455,1,0.9934,"",1]],"ar":1.73623}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":0,"label":"Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_159_1439.jpg","cap":"Pericardial sac in relation to sternum and phrenic nerves","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.44","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7573,0.2512,0.955,0.295]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s061.jpg","cap":"Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint) on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 61","ov":[[0.0989,0.9478,0.2791,0.9967]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s526~sternal-angle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Costal notch for first rib","B. Jugular (suprasternal) notch","C. Costal notches for ribs 2-7","D. Clavicular notch of manubrium","E. Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint).</b> The sternal angle is the ridge at the joint between manubrium and sternal body where the second costal cartilages attach, level with the T4–T5 disc.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and pectoralis major origins; palpable ridge where the 2nd costal cartilages attach</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Plane of the T4-T5 disc: start and end of the aortic arch, tracheal bifurcation</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the jugular notch and slide down the manubrium to the first transverse ridge; the rib cartilage attached at that level is the second.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s526.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 526"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.5048,0.9455,0.8471,0.9934,"",1]],"ar":1.73623,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s061~sternal-angle"}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":0,"label":"Xiphoid process","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_150_102.jpg","cap":"","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7954,0.7245,0.9545,0.7728]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q27.jpg","cap":"Xiphoid process on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 27","ov":[[0.2633,0.9572,0.456,0.9986]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s530~xiphoid-process","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Costal notch for first rib","B. Xiphoid process","C. Clavicular notch of manubrium","D. Costal notches for ribs 2-7","E. Jugular (suprasternal) notch"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Xiphoid process","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Xiphoid process.</b> The xiphoid process is the small lowest part of the sternum, below the sternal body at the xiphisternal joint (level with T9), where the two costal margins meet.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Linea alba and rectus abdominis attach to its front; skin of the epigastric fossa</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sternal slips of the diaphragm and transversus thoracis, which attach to its back</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the costal margins converging in the midline; the small projection at their apex, below the body of the sternum, is the xiphoid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s530.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 530"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.5048,0.9455,0.8771,0.9934,"",1]],"ar":1.73623,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q27~xiphoid-process"}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":1,"label":"First rib","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_750_2300.jpg","cap":"Formation of brachial plexus","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 6.43","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7987,0.7072,0.8571,0.7499]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s064~first-rib","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Twelfth (12th) rib","B. Eleventh rib","C. First rib","D. Head of rib","E. Shaft (body) of rib"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"First rib","a":"C","why":"<b>C. First rib.</b> The first rib is the short, flat, tightly curved rib just under the clavicle, joining T1 and the manubrium, with the subclavian vein and artery crossing its top.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Subclavian vein, anterior scalene, subclavian artery and brachial plexus lower trunk crossing its upper surface</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Cervical pleura over the lung apex, which rises above its inner border</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the manubrium below the clavicle; the rib whose cartilage joins the manubrium just under the clavicular notch, curving back to T1, is the first rib.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s064.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 64"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.0989,0.9365,0.2719,0.992,"",1]],"ar":0.71929}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":1,"label":"Twelfth (12th) rib","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_104_1331.jpg","cap":"Atypical ribs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.3","tb":1,"ov":[[0.2205,0.8735,0.3045,0.9113]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q34~twelfth-rib","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Second rib","B. 9th rib","C. Twelfth (12th) rib","D. Shaft (body) of rib","E. First rib"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Twelfth (12th) rib","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Twelfth (12th) rib.</b> The twelfth rib is the short lowest floating rib, joined only to T12 and ending free in the posterior abdominal wall muscles, with quadratus lumborum attached to its lower border.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Latissimus dorsi, serratus posterior inferior and, medially, erector spinae</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Diaphragm and pleura (pleura dips below its medial part), then the kidney's upper pole</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Count down the ribs at the back or up from the iliac crest; the short lowest rib articulating with T12 is the twelfth.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q34.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 34"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.061,0.8525,0.204,0.9401,"",0],[0.4831,0.9428,0.6561,0.9933,"",1]],"ar":0.805}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":2,"label":"Thoracic vertebra","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_102_1326.jpg","cap":"Thoracic skeleton","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.1","tb":1,"ov":[[0.5136,0.0106,0.7384,0.0551]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s544~thoracic-vertebra","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Tubercle of rib","B. First rib","C. Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)","D. Thoracic vertebra","E. Anulus fibrosus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic vertebra","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Thoracic vertebra.</b> The twelve thoracic vertebrae lie between the cervical and lumbar spine, and each is recognized by its heart-shaped body and facets where the ribs' heads and tubercles attach.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Posterior mediastinum: descending aorta, azygos vein, thoracic duct and esophagus in front of the bodies</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find a rib head and follow it medially; the vertebra it articulates with, whose transverse process supports the rib tubercle, is thoracic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s544.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 544"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.3039,0.043,0.6066,0.1288,"",0],[0.3039,0.1188,0.6349,0.2047,"",0],[0.3303,0.9321,0.534,0.9911,"",1]],"ar":0.67}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":3,"label":"Head of rib","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_106_1333.jpg","cap":"Costovertebral articulations of a typical rib","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.5","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1265,0.2919,0.2942,0.3403]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s532~head-of-rib","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Neck of rib","B. Costal notch for first rib","C. Head of rib","D. Tubercle of rib","E. Costal notches for ribs 2-7"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Head of rib","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Head of rib.</b> The head of a rib is its back end, medial to the neck and tubercle, with two facets for its own vertebral body and the one above.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Radiate ligament, then the sympathetic trunk under the parietal pleura</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Costal facets of its own vertebra and the one above, and the disc between</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the tubercle on the back of the rib; the end medial to it, beyond the narrow neck, is the head.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s532.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 532"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2524,0.9396,0.4254,0.9927,"",1]],"ar":0.75929}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":3,"label":"Articular facet of head of rib","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_104_1331.jpg","cap":"Atypical ribs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.3","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1955,0.3688,0.2784,0.4266]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s536~articular-facet-of-head-of-rib","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Joint of head of rib","B. Neck of rib","C. Head of the 6th rib","D. Articular facet of head of rib","E. Tubercle of rib"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Articular facet of head of rib","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Articular facet of head of rib.</b> The head of a typical rib carries two facets, split by a crest, that meet the body of its own vertebra and the vertebra above at the costovertebral joint.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Joint capsule and the radiate ligament in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Demifacet on the vertebral body, inside the synovial joint of the rib head</dd></dl>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s536.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 536"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2524,0.9392,0.5604,0.9926,"",1]],"ar":0.75357}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":3,"label":"Neck of rib","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_110_1341.jpg","cap":"Costotransverse joints","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.9","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3701,0.1828,0.4478,0.3119]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s534~neck-of-rib","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Neck of rib","B. Articular facet of head of rib","C. Tubercle of rib","D. Joint of head of rib","E. Head of the 6th rib"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Neck of rib","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Neck of rib.</b> The neck of a rib is the narrow bar between its head and tubercle, lying in front of its vertebra's transverse process; ribs 11 and 12 have none.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura, seen from inside the thorax</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transverse process of its own vertebra, which lies directly behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the head and the tubercle; the slender bone between them is the neck.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s534.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 534"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2524,0.9392,0.4254,0.9926,"",1]],"ar":0.75357}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":3,"label":"Tubercle of rib","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_106_1333.jpg","cap":"Costovertebral articulations of a typical rib","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.5","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1146,0.4843,0.2518,0.5324]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s542~tubercle-of-rib","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Costal notches for ribs 2-7","B. Joint of head of rib","C. Articular facet of head of rib","D. Neck of rib","E. Tubercle of rib"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Tubercle of rib","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Tubercle of rib.</b> The tubercle is the knob on the back of a rib where the neck meets the shaft, its facet joining the transverse process of the same-numbered vertebra at the costotransverse joint.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Lateral costotransverse ligament, then erector spinae (deep back muscles)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Tip of the transverse process of the same-numbered vertebra (costotransverse joint)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the rib medially from its angle; the knob just before the narrow neck, resting on the transverse process, is the tubercle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s542.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 542"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2524,0.9392,0.4254,0.9926,"",1]],"ar":0.75357}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":4,"label":"Angle of rib","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_104_1330.jpg","cap":"Typical ribs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.2","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7272,0.0311,0.9279,0.107]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s540~angle-of-rib","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Costal cartilage left 4th rib","B. Neck of rib","C. Costal cartilage of the 3rd rib","D. Angle of rib","E. Shaft (body) of rib"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Angle of rib","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Angle of rib.</b> The angle of the rib is the sharp forward bend of the posterior shaft just lateral to the tubercle; the rib is weakest just in front of it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Iliocostalis (lateral column of erector spinae), which attaches along the rib angles</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Parietal pleura; the intercostal vessels and nerve enter the costal groove at this point</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the tubercle, then follow the shaft laterally until it curves forward; that bend is the angle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s540.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 540"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2524,0.9392,0.4254,0.9926,"",1]],"ar":0.75357}]},{"l":"BON-T1","u":4,"label":"Shaft (body) of rib","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_104_1331.jpg","cap":"Atypical ribs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.3","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6237,0.4009,0.6825,0.438]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s538~shaft-of-rib","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Shaft (body) of rib","B. Tubercle of rib","C. Costal cartilage of the right 4th rib","D. Angle of rib","E. Costal cartilage of right 1st rib"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Shaft (body) of rib","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Shaft (body) of rib.</b> The shaft is the long, curved body of the rib, running from the tubercle around the chest to the costal cartilage, with the costal groove along its inner lower edge.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Serratus anterior, pectoral muscles or latissimus dorsi, depending on the region</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Innermost intercostal, endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura; intercostal bundle in its costal groove</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the costal groove on the inner surface; the long curved bone carrying it is the shaft.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s538.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 538"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2524,0.9392,0.4254,0.9926,"",1]],"ar":0.75357}]},{"l":"BON-A1","u":5,"label":"Lumbar vertebra","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_341_1787.jpg","cap":"Muscles and nerves of posterior abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.97","tb":1,"ov":[[0.775,0.594,0.9502,0.6436]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s546~lumbar-vertebra","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Pubic tubercle","B. Lumbar vertebra","C. Iliac fossa","D. Iliac crest","E. Erector spinae muscle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lumbar vertebra","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lumbar vertebra.</b> A lumbar vertebra has a large kidney-shaped body with no rib facets, long thin transverse processes and a short square spinous process, with psoas major hugging its sides.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Aorta (left) and IVC (inferior vena cava, right) in front; psoas major beside the body</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Erector spinae and multifidus behind, beside the laminae and spinous process</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the psoas major muscles on the slab; the large vertebral body between them, with no ribs attached, is lumbar.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s546.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 546"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.3612,0.0875,0.6079,0.185,"",0],[0.3612,0.1761,0.6307,0.2736,"",0],[0.3303,0.9032,0.5233,0.9853,"",1]],"ar":0.46143}]},{"l":"BON-A1","u":6,"label":"Iliac fossa","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_470_526.jpg","cap":"Components of the pelvic bone","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 5.21","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0769,0.1419,0.1663,0.1852]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s514~iliac-fossa","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)","B. Ala (wing) of left ilium","C. Iliac fossa","D. Iliac crest","E. Pubic tubercle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Iliac fossa","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Iliac fossa.</b> The iliac fossa is the smooth hollow on the inner surface of the iliac wing, below the iliac crest, lined by the iliacus muscle.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Iliacus and iliac fascia; cecum and appendix on the right, sigmoid colon on the left</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Look at the inner side of the ilium; the broad shallow bowl below the crest is the iliac fossa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s514.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 514"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2524,0.9305,0.4254,0.9908,"",1]],"ar":0.65357}]},{"l":"BON-A1","u":6,"label":"Iliac crest","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_316_323.jpg","cap":"Innervation of the anterolateral abdominal wall","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.36","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7637,0.6307,0.9053,0.6774]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s516~iliac-crest","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pubic tubercle","B. Iliac crest","C. Iliac fossa","D. Ala (wing) of left ilium","E. Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Iliac crest","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Iliac crest.</b> The iliac crest is the curved upper border of the ilium running from the anterior to the posterior superior iliac spine, its highest point level with L4.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and subcutaneous fat only, so it is palpable along its length</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the anterior superior iliac spine at the front of the hip and follow the bony ridge backward and upward.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s516.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 516"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2524,0.9304,0.4254,0.9908,"",1]],"ar":0.65286}]},{"l":"BON-A1","u":6,"label":"Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_219_1561.jpg","cap":"Anterolateral abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.7","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7679,0.5995,0.9758,0.6383]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s510.jpg","cap":"Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 510","ov":[[0.2504,0.9285,0.561,0.9928]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q24~anterior-superior-iliac-spine","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pubic tubercle","B. Iliac fossa","C. Ala (wing) of left ilium","D. Iliac crest","E. Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS).</b> The anterior superior iliac spine is the rounded front tip of the iliac crest, where sartorius arises and the inguinal ligament starts its run to the pubic tubercle.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and subcutaneous fat only, so it is easily palpable</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the iliac crest and follow it forward to its rounded anterior tip; the inguinal ligament leaves that point toward the pubic tubercle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q24.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 24"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2767,0.9551,0.5833,0.9958,"",1]],"ar":1.04571}]},{"l":"BON-A1","u":6,"label":"Pubic tubercle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_475_537.jpg","cap":"Pubic symphysis and associated ligaments","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 5.25","tb":1,"ov":[[0.5612,0.102,0.7928,0.1552]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s512~pubic-tubercle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Iliac fossa","B. Pubic tubercle","C. Ala (wing) of left ilium","D. Iliac crest","E. Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pubic tubercle","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pubic tubercle.</b> The pubic tubercle is the small knob on the upper border of the pubis at the lateral end of the pubic crest, where the inguinal ligament attaches.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and fat; the spermatic cord passes just above it from the superficial inguinal ring</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pubic symphysis in the midline and move laterally along the upper border of the pubis to the first knob.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s512.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 512"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2524,0.9304,0.4254,0.9908,"",1]],"ar":0.65286}]},{"l":"BON-A1","u":6,"label":"Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_219_1561.jpg","cap":"Anterolateral abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.7","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7679,0.5995,0.9758,0.6383]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q24.jpg","cap":"Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 24","ov":[[0.2747,0.9531,0.5853,0.9978]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s510~anterior-superior-iliac-spine","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Iliac crest","B. Pubic tubercle","C. Iliac fossa","D. Ala (wing) of left ilium","E. Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS).</b> The anterior superior iliac spine is the rounded front tip of the iliac crest, where sartorius arises and the inguinal ligament starts its run to the pubic tubercle.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and subcutaneous fat only, so it is easily palpable</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the iliac crest and follow it forward to its rounded anterior tip; the inguinal ligament leaves that point toward the pubic tubercle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s510.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 510"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.2524,0.9305,0.559,0.9908,"",1]],"ar":0.65357,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q24~anterior-superior-iliac-spine"}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"BON-T1","h":"Clavicle and sternum","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Manubrium (of sternum)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin, the sternal heads of sternocleidomastoid and pectoralis major</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sternohyoid, sternothyroid, thymus remnant, left brachiocephalic vein, then the aortic arch branches</dd></dl><p>The widest and thickest part of the sternum, at about the level of T3–T4: its superior border has the jugular notch between two clavicular notches, the first costal cartilage attaches just below each clavicular notch, and its lower border joins the body at the sternal angle, where the second costal cartilage attaches. The left brachiocephalic vein and the arch branches lie directly behind it. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the medial ends of the clavicles; the broad bone between them is the manubrium, ending below at the transverse ridge of the sternal angle.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Jugular (suprasternal) notch</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and fascia only, between the sternal heads of the two sternocleidomastoids</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Trachea at the root of the neck, felt in the midline deep to it</dd></dl><p>The shallow, palpable notch in the middle of the superior border of the manubrium, between the two clavicular notches, at about the level of the lower border of T2. The trachea lies deep to it at the root of the neck. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the medial ends of both clavicles; the dip in the midline between them on top of the manubrium is the jugular notch.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_107_1334.jpg\" alt=\"Sternum\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:49.22%;top:27.83%;width:17.57%;height:5.07%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Manubrium (of sternum): Sternum <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.6</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_159_1439.jpg\" alt=\"Pericardial sac in relation to sternum and phrenic nerves\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:75.73%;top:25.12%;width:19.77%;height:4.38%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint): Pericardial sac in relation to sternum and phrenic nerves <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.44</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Clavicular notch of manubrium</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior sternoclavicular ligament and the sternal head of sternocleidomastoid</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sternohyoid origin, then the brachiocephalic vein forming behind the sternoclavicular joint</dd></dl><p>The clavicular notches are the shallow sockets on each upper corner of the manubrium, beside the jugular notch, where the medial ends of the clavicles form the sternoclavicular joints.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Costal notch for first rib</b></p><p>The costal notch for the first rib is the depression on the upper side edge of the manubrium, just below the clavicular notch, where the 1st costal cartilage attaches.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and pectoralis major origins; palpable ridge where the 2nd costal cartilages attach</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Plane of the T4-T5 disc: start and end of the aortic arch, tracheal bifurcation</dd></dl><p>The slight, palpable, forward-projecting ridge at the manubriosternal joint, where the second costal cartilages articulate on each side; it lies at the level of the T4/T5 intervertebral disc. That plane marks the beginning and end of the aortic arch, the tracheal bifurcation, the azygos entering the SVC and the boundary between superior and inferior mediastinum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the jugular notch and slide down the manubrium to the first transverse ridge; the rib cartilage attached at that level is the second.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Xiphoid process</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Linea alba and rectus abdominis attach to its front; skin of the epigastric fossa</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sternal slips of the diaphragm and transversus thoracis, which attach to its back</dd></dl><p>The smallest and most variable part of the sternum, projecting downward from the body at the xiphisternal joint (about T9); it may be pointed, blunt, bifid or perforated, and is cartilaginous in young people and ossifies with age. It sits at the apex of the angle between the two costal margins, with the linea alba and part of the diaphragm attached to it. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the costal margins converging in the midline; the small projection at their apex, below the body of the sternum, is the xiphoid.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Costal notches for ribs 2-7</b></p><p>The costal notches for ribs 2–7 line the side edges of the sternum: the 2nd at the sternal angle, the 3rd–6th on the body, the 7th at the xiphisternal junction.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Manubrium (of sternum)</b>: deep to skin, the sternal heads of sternocleidomastoid and pectoralis major; superficial to sternohyoid, sternothyroid, thymus remnant, left brachiocephalic vein, then the aortic arch branches</li><li><b>Jugular (suprasternal) notch</b>: deep to skin and fascia only, between the sternal heads of the two sternocleidomastoids; superficial to trachea at the root of the neck, felt in the midline deep to it</li><li><b>Clavicular notch of manubrium</b>: deep to anterior sternoclavicular ligament and the sternal head of sternocleidomastoid; superficial to sternohyoid origin, then the brachiocephalic vein forming behind the sternoclavicular joint</li><li><b>Costal notch for first rib</b>: The costal notch for the first rib is the depression on the upper side edge of the manubrium, just below the clavicular notch, where the 1st costal cartilage attaches.</li><li><b>Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)</b>: deep to skin and pectoralis major origins; superficial to plane of the T4-T5 disc: start and end of the aortic arch, tracheal bifurcation</li><li><b>Xiphoid process</b>: deep to linea alba and rectus abdominis attach to its front; superficial to sternal slips of the diaphragm and transversus thoracis, which attach to its back</li><li><b>Costal notches for ribs 2-7</b>: The costal notches for ribs 2–7 line the side edges of the sternum: the 2nd at the sternal angle, the 3rd–6th on the body, the 7th at the xiphisternal junction.</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Manubrium (of sternum)","Jugular (suprasternal) notch","Clavicular notch of manubrium","Costal notch for first rib","Sternal angle (manubriosternal joint)","Xiphoid process","Costal notches for ribs 2-7"]},{"l":"BON-T1","h":"Ribs, first to twelfth","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>First rib</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Subclavian vein, anterior scalene, subclavian artery and brachial plexus lower trunk crossing its upper surface</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Cervical pleura over the lung apex, which rises above its inner border</dd></dl><p>The broadest and most sharply curved rib, with flat superior and inferior surfaces; its head has a single facet for T1, it has a neck and tubercle, and its superior surface bears the scalene tubercle with a groove for the subclavian vein in front and a groove for the subclavian artery behind. Its costal cartilage joins the manubrium just below the clavicle. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the manubrium below the clavicle; the rib whose cartilage joins the manubrium just under the clavicular notch, curving back to T1, is the first rib.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Twelfth (12th) rib</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Latissimus dorsi, serratus posterior inferior and, medially, erector spinae</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Diaphragm and pleura (pleura dips below its medial part), then the kidney's upper pole</dd></dl><p>The last floating rib, short and narrow with a slight curve: its head has a single facet for T12, it has no neck or tubercle, and its tapered anterior end ends free in the posterior abdominal wall muscles. Quadratus lumborum attaches to its inferior border. <i>Finding it:</i> Count down the ribs at the back or up from the iliac crest; the short lowest rib articulating with T12 is the twelfth.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_750_2300.jpg\" alt=\"Formation of brachial plexus\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:79.87%;top:70.72%;width:5.84%;height:4.27%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>First rib: Formation of brachial plexus <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 6.43</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_104_1331.jpg\" alt=\"Atypical ribs\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:22.05%;top:87.35%;width:8.40%;height:3.78%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Twelfth (12th) rib: Atypical ribs <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.3</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>First rib</b>: deep to subclavian vein, anterior scalene, subclavian artery and brachial plexus lower trunk crossing its upper surface; superficial to cervical pleura over the lung apex, which rises above its inner border</li><li><b>Twelfth (12th) rib</b>: deep to latissimus dorsi, serratus posterior inferior and, medially, erector spinae; superficial to diaphragm and pleura (pleura dips below its medial part), then the kidney's upper pole</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["First rib","Twelfth (12th) rib"]},{"l":"BON-T1","h":"Thoracic vertebrae, discs and the T4 level","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Thoracic vertebra</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Posterior mediastinum: descending aorta, azygos vein, thoracic duct and esophagus in front of the bodies</dd></dl><p>Has a heart-shaped body with costal facets (usually demifacets) on its sides for the rib heads, transverse processes with facets for the rib tubercles (usually T1–T10), a small round vertebral foramen and a long spinous process sloping inferiorly. Ribs articulating at its sides identify it on any specimen or slab. <i>Finding it:</i> Find a rib head and follow it medially; the vertebra it articulates with, whose transverse process supports the rib tubercle, is thoracic.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_102_1326.jpg\" alt=\"Thoracic skeleton\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:51.36%;top:1.06%;width:22.48%;height:4.45%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Thoracic vertebra: Thoracic skeleton <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.1</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Thoracic vertebra</b>: deep to posterior mediastinum: descending aorta, azygos vein, thoracic duct and esophagus in front of the bodies</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Thoracic vertebra"]},{"l":"BON-T1","h":"Back of a rib and its joints with the vertebra","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Head of rib</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Radiate ligament, then the sympathetic trunk under the parietal pleura</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Costal facets of its own vertebra and the one above, and the disc between</dd></dl><p>The posterior end of the rib that articulates with the vertebral bodies; in a typical rib it is wedge-shaped with two articular facets separated by a crest, the lower facet for the body of the same-numbered vertebra and the upper facet for the vertebra above. The 1st, 10th, 11th and 12th ribs usually have a single facet. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the tubercle on the back of the rib; the end medial to it, beyond the narrow neck, is the head.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Articular facet of head of rib</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Joint capsule and the radiate ligament in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Demifacet on the vertebral body, inside the synovial joint of the rib head</dd></dl><p>The head of a typical rib carries two facets, split by a crest, that meet the body of its own vertebra and the vertebra above at the costovertebral joint.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_106_1333.jpg\" alt=\"Costovertebral articulations of a typical rib\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:12.65%;top:29.19%;width:16.77%;height:4.84%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Head of rib: Costovertebral articulations of a typical rib <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.5</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_104_1331.jpg\" alt=\"Atypical ribs\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:19.55%;top:36.88%;width:8.29%;height:5.78%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Articular facet of head of rib: Atypical ribs <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.3</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Neck of rib</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura, seen from inside the thorax</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transverse process of its own vertebra, which lies directly behind it</dd></dl><p>The flattened, constricted segment between the head and the tubercle that lies in front of the transverse process of its vertebra; its upper border carries a sharp crest. The 11th and 12th ribs have no neck. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the head and the tubercle; the slender bone between them is the neck.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Tubercle of rib</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Lateral costotransverse ligament, then erector spinae (deep back muscles)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Tip of the transverse process of the same-numbered vertebra (costotransverse joint)</dd></dl><p>Posterior projection at the junction of the neck and shaft: its medial articular part has a facet for the tip of the transverse process of the same-numbered vertebra (costotransverse joint) and its lateral rough part is for ligaments. The 11th and 12th ribs lack a tubercle. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the rib medially from its angle; the knob just before the narrow neck, resting on the transverse process, is the tubercle.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Head of rib</b>: deep to radiate ligament, then the sympathetic trunk under the parietal pleura; superficial to costal facets of its own vertebra and the one above, and the disc between</li><li><b>Articular facet of head of rib</b>: deep to joint capsule and the radiate ligament in front; superficial to demifacet on the vertebral body, inside the synovial joint of the rib head</li><li><b>Neck of rib</b>: deep to endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura, seen from inside the thorax; superficial to transverse process of its own vertebra, which lies directly behind it</li><li><b>Tubercle of rib</b>: deep to lateral costotransverse ligament, then erector spinae (deep back muscles); superficial to tip of the transverse process of the same-numbered vertebra (costotransverse joint)</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Head of rib","Articular facet of head of rib","Neck of rib","Tubercle of rib"]},{"l":"BON-T1","h":"Rib shaft to the sternum","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Angle of rib</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Iliocostalis (lateral column of erector spinae), which attaches along the rib angles</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Parietal pleura; the intercostal vessels and nerve enter the costal groove at this point</dd></dl><p>The point on the posterior shaft, a short distance lateral to the tubercle, where the rib turns sharply anterolaterally; a rough line there marks the lateral limit of attachment of the deep back muscles. The rib is weakest just anterior to its angle. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the tubercle, then follow the shaft laterally until it curves forward; that bend is the angle.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Shaft (body) of rib</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Serratus anterior, pectoral muscles or latissimus dorsi, depending on the region</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Innermost intercostal, endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura; intercostal bundle in its costal groove</dd></dl><p>The longest part of the rib, thin, flat and curved, running from the tubercle around the side of the chest to the costal cartilage; it has a rounded superior border and a sharp inferior border with the costal groove on its inner surface, and it includes the angle posteriorly. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the costal groove on the inner surface; the long curved bone carrying it is the shaft.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_104_1330.jpg\" alt=\"Typical ribs\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:72.72%;top:3.11%;width:20.07%;height:7.59%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Angle of rib: Typical ribs <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.2</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_104_1331.jpg\" alt=\"Atypical ribs\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:62.37%;top:40.09%;width:5.88%;height:3.71%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Shaft (body) of rib: Atypical ribs <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.3</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Angle of rib</b>: deep to iliocostalis (lateral column of erector spinae), which attaches along the rib angles; superficial to parietal pleura</li><li><b>Shaft (body) of rib</b>: deep to serratus anterior, pectoral muscles or latissimus dorsi, depending on the region; superficial to innermost intercostal, endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"5","ks":["Angle of rib","Shaft (body) of rib"]},{"l":"BON-A1","h":"Lumbar vertebra and back muscle","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Lumbar vertebra</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Aorta (left) and IVC (inferior vena cava, right) in front; psoas major beside the body</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Erector spinae and multifidus behind, beside the laminae and spinous process</dd></dl><p>Has a large kidney-shaped body, no costal facets, long thin transverse processes, a short thick quadrangular spinous process projecting straight backward and a triangular vertebral foramen. On abdominal slabs it is flanked by the psoas major muscles, with the aorta at its left front and the IVC at its right front. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the psoas major muscles on the slab; the large vertebral body between them, with no ribs attached, is lumbar.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_341_1787.jpg\" alt=\"Muscles and nerves of posterior abdominal wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:77.50%;top:59.40%;width:17.52%;height:4.96%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Lumbar vertebra: Muscles and nerves of posterior abdominal wall <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.97</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Lumbar vertebra</b>: deep to aorta (left) and IVC (inferior vena cava, right) in front; superficial to erector spinae and multifidus behind, beside the laminae and spinous process</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Lumbar vertebra"]},{"l":"BON-A1","h":"Hip bone landmarks","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Iliac fossa</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Iliacus and iliac fascia; cecum and appendix on the right, sigmoid colon on the left</dd></dl><p>The large smooth hollow on the inner surface of the ala (wing) of the ilium, below the iliac crest, forming the lateral wall of the false pelvis. Iliacus arises from it. <i>Finding it:</i> Look at the inner side of the ilium; the broad shallow bowl below the crest is the iliac fossa.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Iliac crest</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and subcutaneous fat only, so it is palpable along its length</dd></dl><p>The thickened, curved superior border of the ilium running from the anterior superior iliac spine to the posterior superior iliac spine; its highest point is usually at about the L4 level, and the abdominal wall muscles and quadratus lumborum attach along it. The tubercle of the crest lies about 5 cm behind the anterior superior iliac spine. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the anterior superior iliac spine at the front of the hip and follow the bony ridge backward and upward.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_470_526.jpg\" alt=\"Components of the pelvic bone\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:7.69%;top:14.19%;width:8.94%;height:4.33%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Iliac fossa: Components of the pelvic bone <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 5.21</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_316_323.jpg\" alt=\"Innervation of the anterolateral abdominal wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:76.37%;top:63.07%;width:14.16%;height:4.67%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Iliac crest: Innervation of the anterolateral abdominal wall <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.36</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and subcutaneous fat only, so it is easily palpable</dd></dl><p>The prominent projection at the anterior end of the iliac crest, palpable at the front of the hip; the inguinal ligament runs from it to the pubic tubercle and the sartorius arises from it. The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve usually passes just medial to it, deep to the inguinal ligament. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the iliac crest and follow it forward to its rounded anterior tip; the inguinal ligament leaves that point toward the pubic tubercle.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Pubic tubercle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and fat; the spermatic cord passes just above it from the superficial inguinal ring</dd></dl><p>Small projection on the superior border of the body of the pubis, at the lateral end of the pubic crest close to the pubic symphysis; the inguinal ligament attaches to it, and the superficial inguinal ring lies just superolateral to it. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pubic symphysis in the midline and move laterally along the upper border of the pubis to the first knob.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Iliac fossa</b>: deep to iliacus and iliac fascia</li><li><b>Iliac crest</b>: deep to skin and subcutaneous fat only, so it is palpable along its length</li><li><b>Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)</b>: deep to skin and subcutaneous fat only, so it is easily palpable</li><li><b>Pubic tubercle</b>: deep to skin and fat</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Iliac fossa","Iliac crest","Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)","Pubic tubercle"]}]};
